\description{
Encapsulates the choropleth boilerplate and goes beyond a single style.
Auto-detects the polygon vs \code{sf} backend, applies \code{\link[=theme_world_map]{theme_world_map()}}, and
projects the map (Equal Earth by default). Classes are the default because
a continuous fill on a skewed indicator hides almost all the variation;
binning is the honest default for choropleths, and quantiles the safe
choice for a general audience.
}
\section{Missing data is not zero}{

The default grey reads as "low" to many people, which is exactly wrong for
"unknown". \code{na_style = "hatched"} draws diagonal hatching instead --
unambiguous, and it survives greyscale printing. \code{"omit"} leaves a hole,
which is honest but can be mistaken for ocean. Whichever you pick,
\code{footnote = "auto"} states the count in words:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{world_map(mapdf, gdp_per_capita, na_style = "hatched", footnote = "auto")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{\link[=coverage_map]{coverage_map()}} goes further and maps availability itself.
}

\section{Projections on the polygon backend}{

The polygon backend (the default of \code{\link[=attach_geometry]{attach_geometry()}}, \code{\link[=world_data]{world_data()}} and
\code{\link[=join_world]{join_world()}}) keeps its frame in longitude and latitude and is projected
when it is drawn. Where \code{sf} can be loaded that is
\code{\link[ggplot2:ggsf]{ggplot2::coord_sf()}} with \code{default_crs = sf::st_crs(4326)}, so any layer
you add in longitude and latitude is projected with the map, point by
point: the map's own outlines are dense, so a segment is drawn straight
between its two projected ends rather than re-interpolated, and a long line
of your own needs points along it to follow the projection. Where it
cannot, Equal Earth is computed by the package itself, on the sphere, and
the vertices are drawn in metres under \code{\link[ggplot2:coord_fixed]{ggplot2::coord_fixed()}}; place your
own layers on that map with \code{\link[=project_lonlat]{project_lonlat()}}, and zoom with \code{\link[=zoom_map]{zoom_map()}},
which keeps the projection where \code{coord_quickmap(xlim, ylim)} would replace
it. Another projection without \code{sf} falls back to that Equal Earth with a
warning (class \code{countryatlas_projection_fallback}). \code{"orthographic"} draws
through \code{\link[ggplot2:coord_map]{ggplot2::coord_map()}} and needs \code{mapproj}. \code{\link[=map_provenance]{map_provenance()}}
records which of these drew the map: \code{"equal_earth"}, \code{"equal_earth (spherical, built-in)"} or \code{"none"}.
}

\section{Choosing a classification}{

The classification changes what readers conclude, and not by a little.
Brewer & Pickle's 56-subject study over nine map series found \strong{quantiles}
among the best methods for general choropleth reading, and natural breaks
(Jenks) below 70\% as accurate -- the opposite of the common GIS default.
\code{style = "quantile"} is therefore the safe choice for a general audience.
Jenks earns its place on strongly clustered distributions, where quantiles
would split a natural group across two colours. Use \code{\link[=classify_compare]{classify_compare()}} to
see the difference on your own data before committing.
}

